Every partial recursive function of finitely many natural numbers has a very strict λ-definition: an approximable map from the universal argument domain to N that returns the function's value on total arguments, returns bottom where the function is undefined, and returns bottom on any argument-like input with bottom among its first coordinates.

The argument is induction on the partial-recursive derivation. A primitive-recursive function is λ-definable by the primitive-recursive case. For composition, the inductive hypotheses give λ-definitions of the outer function and of the inner functions, which the composition construction combines. For unbounded search, the inductive hypothesis gives a λ-definition of the body, which the search construction turns into a λ-definition of the search.
